AutoHarness's desktop shell (`crates/ui-gpui`) is derived from diri:

- `src/theme.rs` ports the design system from `crates/diri-ui/src/tokens.rs`
  and `crates/diri-ui/src/status.rs`: type scale, corner radii, spacing and
  metrics, the semantic colour model, fill opacities, the status vocabulary,
  the Motion constants, and the wall-clock AnimationPhase math.
- `src/palette.rs` ports the command palette's action model and ranking from
  `crates/diri-app/src/palette.rs`, including the penalized-invisible-keyword
  scheme and curated-order tiebreak.

- `src/ansi.rs` takes the terminal colour model (`TermColor`, `TermStyle`)
  from `crates/diri-proto/src/grid.rs`.
- `src/query_editor.rs` and `src/fuzzy.rs` are transplanted essentially
  verbatim from `crates/diri-app/src/query_editor.rs` and
  `crates/diri-app/src/fuzzy.rs`, including their tests.
- `src/motion.rs` ports the monotonic critically damped seam response and
  260 ms duration from `crates/diri-app/src/seam.rs`; floating-surface and tab
  transitions adapt the 160 ms and 190 ms recipes from `diri-ui` and the diri
  inspector while preserving GPUI's reduced-motion behavior.
- `assets/icons/*.svg` are diri's 24x24 line-icon family, copied unchanged
  from `crates/diri-ui/assets/icons/`. `src/icon.rs` adapts
  `crates/diri-ui/src/icon.rs`: the embedded `AssetSource`, the optical size
  scale and the tintable `Icon` element carry across, while diri's SF Symbol
  compatibility bridge is dropped because AutoHarness never used those names.
  `assets/icons/arrow-left.svg` is drawn in the same family style.
- `assets/icons/brand-claude.svg`, `brand-openai.svg`, `brand-cursor.svg`
  and `brand-gemini.svg` embed the 24x24 brand-mark path data from
  `crates/diri-ui/src/brand.rs` (`CLAUDE_PATH`, `OPENAI_PATH`, `CURSOR_PATH`,
  `GEMINI_PATH`), rendered through the shared `Icon` element instead of
  diri's canvas rasterizer. The Claude clay and Gemini blue tints in
  `src/theme.rs` come from diri's `Palette`.
- `assets/icons/brand-copilot.svg`, `brand-kimi.svg`, `brand-opencode.svg`
  and `brand-pi.svg` are from Simple Icons
  (<https://simpleicons.org>, CC0 1.0).
  All brand marks identify the respective third-party products and remain
  trademarks of their owners.
- `components::floating_surface` adapts diri's shared floating-surface recipe
  so every popover in the shell has one geometry and one material.
- `src/onboarding.rs` follows diri's readiness surfacing — every precondition
  listed whether or not it passes, an unavailable one dimmed with the reason
  beside it — applied to AutoHarness's own preconditions rather than to
  diri's agent catalog.

`crates/pty` is transplanted wholesale from diri's `crates/diri-engine`:

- `src/pty.rs`, `src/pty/unix.rs`, `src/pty/unsupported.rs`, `src/log.rs`,
  `src/screen.rs`, `src/detect/*` and `src/status/*` carry across essentially
  unchanged, with their tests. These are the parts that know about file
  descriptors, terminals and screen rules rather than about diri.
- `manifests/*.json` are diri's agent detection manifests, copied unchanged
  from `Sources/DirijorCore/Resources/manifests/`. They are data: the rules
  that decide whether an agent is working, waiting or done.
- `src/types.rs` replaces the `diri-proto` wire types the status reducer
  depended on (`SessionStatus`, `NeedsInputDetail` and friends) with plain
  domain types, since AutoHarness has no Swift daemon to stay wire-compatible
  with. diri's vendor-specific `NeedsInputSource` variants collapse to how the
  signal was learned rather than whose hook produced it.

Concepts adapted rather than transplanted, listed so the debt is visible even
where no code was copied:

- The three-pane cockpit shape — sidebar, coordinator, collapsible workbench
  and inspector, with draggable seams — follows diri's layout thinking.
- The surface vocabulary: overview, history, worktrees, notifications, and
  settings as first-class panels reached from one toolbar rather than from a
  slash-command vocabulary.
- The idea that a background unit of work earns an alert while a foreground
  one does not (`src/attention.rs`). The reducer, its triggers, its replay
  suppression, and its settings gates are AutoHarness's own.

These are adapted to AutoHarness's domain — runs, nodes, worktrees and engines
rather than sessions and agents — and reimplemented against its own state.
AutoHarness additionally renders the status marks with motion, which diri
computes but deliberately does not schedule frames for.

AutoHarness is not a copy of diri and does not share its architecture. The
daemon, the event ledger, the Seatbelt sandbox, the worktree index and its
fail-closed reclaim, the router and detector, the engine adapters, and the
update verification pipeline are AutoHarness's own and have no diri
counterpart. Where the two disagree, AutoHarness's safety architecture wins:
no external write, sandbox failure closed, persist before broadcast, and no
mutation of the user's base checkout.
